(** The square root of 2 is irrational, bottom up: each result is proved
    before the one that uses it, so the headline, [sqrt2_irrational], comes
    last. sqrt2_irrational.exp proves the same top down. In three steps:
    1. [no_whole_solution]: there are no whole numbers [p] and [q != 0]
       with [p * p = 2 * (q * q)], by the classic infinite descent.
    2. [sqrt2_not_rational]: no rational number squares to 2, since a
       fraction [n / d] that did would give such whole numbers.
    3. [sqrt2_irrational]: [sqrt 2] is irrational, since a rational equal
       to it would square to 2.
    [lia], [nia] and [lra], from the prelude, prove the arithmetic.

    The proofs are in sqrt2_irrational_bottom_up.exp; this file defines
    [irrational], what they are about. If the program builds, the theorems
    are proved: every step is checked, and nothing in it is assumed
    ([Axiom], [Admitted]) or left to run time ([Any]). Steps 1 and 2 use no
    axioms at all. Step 3 is about Stdlib's real numbers, which are built on
    axioms (classical logic and functional extensionality);
    [Print Assumptions sqrt2_irrational] lists them. *)

From Stdlib Require Import QArith Reals Qreals Lra.
Close Scope Q_scope.

(** A real number is irrational when no rational equals it. [Q2R] turns a
    rational into a real. *)
irrational (x : R) := ~ exists q : Q, Q2R q = x.
